UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 27, Lesson 4: Explaining a process orally', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"An oral explanation walks listeners through stages in order, using signal words so the how/why is easy to follow.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSpeak a 20-second how-explanation for making toast using First/Next/Then.\n\nFEEDBACK\nKeep three clear stages; avoid skipping the heating step.\n\nCHECK\nSpeaking facts out of order makes the process hard to picture.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nGive a 45–60 second oral explanation of a simple process. Use at least three signal words, face the audience and write the stage list you followed.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"An oral explanation walks listeners through stages in order, using signal words so the how/why is easy to follow.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01","EN2-HANDW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Give a 45–60 second oral explanation of a simple process. Use at least three signal words, face the audience and write the stage list you followed. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: \"First the seed takes in water. Next it swells. Then the root grows down. Finally the shoot grows up.\" Signal words mark the sequence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Speaking facts out of order makes the process hard to picture. Correction: Keep three clear stages; avoid skipping the heating step.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Speaking facts out of order makes the process hard to picture. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"An oral explanation walks listeners through stages in order, using signal words so the how/why is easy to follow. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["An oral explanation walks listeners through stages in order, using signal words so the how/why is easy to follow.","Speaking facts out of order makes the process hard to picture.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Explain the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Speaking facts out of order makes the process hard to picture.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to an independent example\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Keep three clear stages; avoid skipping the heating step. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["Keep three clear stages; avoid skipping the heating step.","Speaking facts out of order makes the process hard to picture.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Speak a 20-second how-explanation for making toast using First/Next/Then.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["The most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Underline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Keep three clear stages; avoid skipping the heating step. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["Keep three clear stages; avoid skipping the heating step.","A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Speaking facts out of order makes the process hard to picture.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a co\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["The new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Make one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant work with accurate reasoning and a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating the central skill, explaining the particular words or evidence that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: \"First the seed takes in water. Next it swells. Then the root grows down. Finally the shoot grows up.\" Signal words mark the sequence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change meaning or accuracy, not just make the text longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected example and purpose-linked explanation.","question":"Name one likely error in using this skill, correct it and explain why the correction helps.","sample_answer":"Common error: Speaking facts out of order makes the process hard to picture. Correction: Keep three clear stages; avoid skipping the heating step.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w27-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise oral explanation.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use oral explanation with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify order and explain why it matters.","I can practise signal words independently on a new prompt.","I can notice audience needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"An oral explanation walks listeners through stages in order, using signal words so the how/why is easy to follow. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["An oral explanation walks listeners through stages in order, using signal words so the how/why is easy to follow.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"First the seed takes in water. Next it swells. Then the root grows down. Finally the shoot grows up.\" Signal words mark the sequence.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSpeak a 20-second how-explanation for making toast using First/Next/Then.\n\nFEEDBACK\nKeep three clear stages; avoid skipping the heating step.","explain":"An oral explanation walks listeners through stages in order, using signal words so the how/why is easy to follow.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence,